      "text": "We are given that $(4, 5)$ and $(8, 17)$ lie on the line $y = ax + b$. Substituting the coordinates of $(4, 5)$ into the equation $y = ax + b$, we obtain the equation:\n\n\\[\n5 = 4a + b  \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, substituting the coordinates of $(8, 17)$ into the equation $y = ax + b$, we get:\n\n\\[\n17 = 8a + b  \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n\\]\n\nTo find the values of $a$ and $b$, we subtract Equation 1 from Equation 2:\n\n\\[\n(17 = 8a + b) - (5 = 4a + b) \n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n17 - 5 = 8a + b - (4a + b)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n12 = 4a\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 4:\n\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have $a = 3$, we substitute it back into Equation 1 to find $b$:\n\n\\[\n5 = 4(3) + b\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5 = 12 + b\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb = 5 - 12\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb = -7\n\\]\n\nHence, $a - b = 3 - (-7) = 3 + 7 = 10$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]\n\nTo check our work, we can substitute $a = 3$ and $b = -7$ into the initial line equation $y = ax + b$ to ensure it passes through both given points:\n\nFor $(4, 5)$:\n\n\\[\ny = 3(4) - 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5 = 12 - 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5 = 5\n\\]\n\nFor $(8, 17)$:\n\n\\[\ny = 3(8) - 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n17 = 24 - 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n17 = 17\n\\]\n\nSince both points satisfy the equation, our solution is confirmed to be correct.",
